On the Delaney/ESOL dataset (1,128 molecules, 80/20 split, measured solubility as target),
  Random Forest consistently outperformed Linear Regression**, regardless of which feature set was used:

| Features | Linear R² | RF R² | Winner |
|---|---|---|---|
| Descriptors | 0.772 | 0.866 | RF |
| Morgan fingerprints | 0.003 | 0.716 | RF |
| Descriptors + Morgan | 0.024 | 0.870 | RF |

Random Forest on descriptors + Morgan fingerprints was the best-performing model overall** (test R² = 0.870, RMSE = 0.785),
with RF on descriptors alone a close second (R² = 0.866). This suggests the fingerprint bits carry some genuine additional 
signal for RF, though the improvement over descriptors-alone (0.866 → 0.870) is small enough that it could just be noise
from a single train/test split rather than a reliable effect.

Linear Regression could not handle the high-dimensional fingerprint data. With 2,048 fingerprint features and only
902 training molecules, Linear Regression overfit severely — train R² of 0.979–1.000 collapsed 
to test R² near zero whenever fingerprints were included. This is a textbook case of a linear model with too many
parameters relative to training examples; it memorized the training set instead of learning a generalizable relationship.
  Linear Regression only produced a usable model when restricted to the 10 hand-crafted descriptors (R² = 0.772), where the feature-to-sample 
  ratio is far more reasonable.

Takeaway for this dataset, Random Forest is the clearly better model, and the gap is driven specifically by how each 
model handles high-dimensional fingerprint features — Random Forest's tree-based splits aren't thrown off by many weakly-informative 
  binary features the way ordinary least squares is. Molecular descriptors alone are a strong, nearly sufficient feature set for either model; 
fingerprints only help when paired with a model (like RF) that can tolerate their dimensionality.

Limitations worth noting before treating this as final: these numbers come from one random 80/20 split, 
  so small differences (e.g., 0.866 vs. 0.870) aren't meaningful on their own. Random splits can also overstate performance for molecular data, since structurally
similar molecules can land in both train and test sets; a scaffold split would be a stronger test of real generalization. 
  Neither model was hyperparameter-tuned, so this is a baseline comparison, not necessarily each model's best possible performance.
